<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhaja Govindam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as भज गोविन्दम्</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Bhaja Govindam&quot; (&quot;praise Govinda&quot;, or &quot;seek Govinda&quot;), also called &quot;Moha Mudgara&quot; (&quot;destroyer of illusion&quot;), is a popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> devotional poem in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> traditionally attributed to Adi Shankara. Its opening stanza is a refrain repeated after each verse, as Swami Chinmayananda describes it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Bhaja Govindam</b>&quot; (&quot;praise Govinda&quot;, or &quot;seek Govinda&quot;), also called &quot;Moha Mudgara&quot; (&quot;destroyer of illusion&quot;), is a popular Hindu devotional poem in Sanskrit traditionally attributed to Adi Shankara.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bhaja-govindam">Bhaja Govindam</a></li></ul></section>
